scope: |
  Planned. The claim was widened after reading the code, and the reason is the plan:
  the static-frontend path CANNOT EXPRESS a signal-URL fetch at all, so this is not an
  emitter-only change. FetchUrlSignal carries `fetchUrl: String` with no `urlId`, and the
  v1 fetch-plugin implements `fetchUrlSignal` but has no `fetchUrlSignalTo` site — that
  primitive exists only in the JS runtime and v2's UiNativePlugin. So:
    1. Primitives.scala — FetchUrlSignal gains `urlId: Option[String]`, mirroring the
       `headersId` that tui-fetch-headers just added; `fetchUrl` stays as the static form.
    2. FetchIntrinsics.scala — a `fetchUrlSignalTo` site that builds it.
    3. TuiEmitter — resolve the URL from the signal store at FETCH time and re-fetch when
       it changes, exactly as the tick already does (and as headers now do).
    4. TuiEmitterTest — the gate.
  Additive for every other static emitter: an ignored Option field.
next: cargo integration tests in a NEW file TuiFetchCargoTest.scala — see note2 — then smoke-ci, then coord-release
note: |
  tui-fetch-post added to THIS claim rather than a new one, and implemented on the same
  branch — flagged because it bends "one change = one branch". Both items are the same
  reporter's screen and both live in TuiEmitter.scala, which this claim already holds; a
  second claim would collide with my own paths, and a dependent branch would give you a
  conflicting merge of one file instead of two clean commits. Each item keeps its own spec
  and its own commit. Say the word in the room and I will split them.
note2: |
  The cargo tests go in a NEW file, TuiFetchCargoTest.scala, rather than into
  TuiCargoSmokeTest.scala which tui-widget-compile-coverage holds. Not a workaround for the
  guard — the better layout. That holder's whole task is adding compile coverage to that
  same 200-line file, so two agents appending to it is exactly the collision the path guard
  exists to prevent; splitting the fetch cargo tests out by feature removes the collision
  permanently instead of scheduling it. Asked twice in the room first; proceeding rather
  than sitting on finished code. Happy to fold them back in later if you prefer one file.
